  "names": {
    "order": "given family family",
    "conventions": "Most Brazilians carry the mother's family name and then the father's (Ana Clara Oliveira Santos), and are usually known by the last one; married women often add the husband's surname. Compound first names are common (João Pedro, Maria Clara, Ana Paula), and recent favourites include Miguel, Arthur, Heitor, Helena and Alice. Everyday life runs on first names and nicknames, even at work. Immigrant families keep their own surnames: Italian across São Paulo, German in Santa Catarina, Japanese in Liberdade and the São Paulo interior, Lebanese and Syrian in the centre of São Paulo, and Azorean names (Silveira, Bittencourt, Ávila, Goulart) on the island of Florianópolis.",
